<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agudás</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afro-Brazilian returnees, Amaros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Agudás are the descendants of Afro-Brazilians, many of them freed slaves, and Brazilian traders who settled on the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in the 19th century. They brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, Portuguese surnames and Brazilian-style architecture to towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, and formed a distinct community that was influential in commerce and in early colonial administration.</p></div>
